<section class="bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 py-16">
  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
    <h1 class="text-4xl sm:text-5xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white mb-6 text-center"><%= post.title %></h1>
    <p class="text-sm text-slate-500 dark:text-slate-400 text-center mb-10">Published on <time datetime="<%= post.date %>"><%= post.date %></time> · Updated on <time datetime="<%= post.dateModified %>"><%= post.dateModified %></time> · By <%= post.author %></p>

    <%- include('../partials/ad-unit', { adSlot: 'blog-transcripts-top' }) %>

    <div class="prose dark:prose-invert max-w-none">
      <p class="text-lg text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
        <strong>Short answer:</strong> On desktop, click the three-dot menu under the video title and choose <strong>Show transcript</strong>. A panel opens on the right with timestamped text you can copy. If you own the video, YouTube Studio lets you download the actual caption file as SRT or VTT.
      </p>
      <p class="text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 leading-relaxed mt-4">Most YouTube videos already have a transcript. The feature has existed since 2018 and most people have never noticed it.</p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white mt-10 mb-4">Method 1: The built-in transcript panel</h2>
      <p class="text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">This works on any public video that has captions — either added by the creator or auto-generated by YouTube, which covers the large majority.</p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white mt-8 mb-3">On desktop</h3>
      <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
        <li>Open the video</li>
        <li>Click <strong>...more</strong> below the video title to expand the description</li>
        <li>Look for the <strong>Show transcript</strong> button</li>
        <li>A panel opens on the right with the full timestamped text</li>
        <li>Click any line to jump to that moment in the video</li>
      </ol>
      <p class="text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 leading-relaxed mt-4"><cite class="text-slate-500 dark:text-slate-400 not-italic">To copy the full transcript, toggle off timestamps using the button at the top of the panel, then select all and copy.</cite></p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white mt-8 mb-3">On mobile</h3>
      <p class="text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">This is where people get stuck. <cite class="text-slate-500 dark:text-slate-400 not-italic">The YouTube mobile app does not show a "Show transcript" button — this is one of the biggest sources of confusion.</cite></p>
      <p class="text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 leading-relaxed mt-4">The workaround: <cite class="text-slate-500 dark:text-slate-400 not-italic">open the video in your mobile browser rather than the app, tap Show more in the description, then tap Show transcript.</cite> It is fiddly on a small screen, but it works.</p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white mt-8 mb-3">Changing language</h3>
      <p class="text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 leading-relaxed"><cite class="text-slate-500 dark:text-slate-400 not-italic">Many videos have multilingual transcripts. Once the panel is open, a dropdown lets you switch languages, and YouTube will show the transcript in your selected language.</cite></p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white mt-8 mb-3">The limitations, stated plainly</h3>
      <p class="text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 leading-relaxed"><cite class="text-slate-500 dark:text-slate-400 not-italic">This only works for videos with captions, is not available where captions are disabled, and is manual only — there is no way to bulk-export or automate it.</cite></p>
      <p class="text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 leading-relaxed mt-4"><cite class="text-slate-500 dark:text-slate-400 not-italic">Live streams in progress do not have transcripts, and neither do videos so new that YouTube has not finished processing them.</cite></p>
      <p class="text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 leading-relaxed mt-4">Also worth knowing: <cite class="text-slate-500 dark:text-slate-400 not-italic">copying transcript text is not the same as downloading a caption file. Viewers generally get copy access; channel owners get actual caption downloads through YouTube Studio.</cite></p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white mt-10 mb-4">Method 2: YouTube Studio (for your own videos)</h2>
      <p class="text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">If you own the channel, you get the real files rather than copied text.</p>
      <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-4">
        <li>Open YouTube Studio</li>
        <li>Go to <strong>Subtitles</strong> in the left menu</li>
        <li>Select the video</li>
        <li><cite class="text-slate-500 dark:text-slate-400 not-italic">Click the Download menu and choose .srt or .vtt</cite></li>
      </ol>
      <p class="text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 leading-relaxed mt-4">This gives you a properly formatted caption file with intact timing — the thing you actually want if you are going to edit, translate, or republish the captions.</p>
      <p class="text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 leading-relaxed mt-4">Which format to pick: SRT for almost everything; VTT if the captions are going into an HTML5 player on your own site. See <a href="/blog/subtitle-formats-compared" class="text-sky-600 dark:text-sky-400 hover:underline">Subtitle Formats: SRT vs VTT vs ASS</a> for the full comparison.</p>
      <p class="text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 leading-relaxed mt-4">This is also the correct route if you want to improve your auto-generated captions. Download the SRT, fix the errors in a text editor, and upload the corrected version. Auto-captions handle accents, technical terms, and proper nouns poorly, and a corrected transcript is meaningfully better for both viewers and search.</p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white mt-10 mb-4">Method 3: The YouTube Data API</h2>
      <p class="text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">For developers who need this at scale, YouTube provides an official Captions API as part of the YouTube Data API v3.</p>
      <p class="text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 leading-relaxed mt-4">The important constraint: the API only lets you download caption tracks for videos you own or have been granted access to. It is built for channel management, not for pulling transcripts from arbitrary videos.</p>
      <p class="text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 leading-relaxed mt-4">If your use case is "get transcripts from other people's videos programmatically," the official API will not do it — and that is a deliberate design decision, not an oversight.</p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white mt-10 mb-4">A note on third-party transcript tools</h2>
      <p class="text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">Plenty of sites offer to fetch a transcript from any URL. Some are convenient and clean up the text nicely.</p>
      <p class="text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 leading-relaxed mt-4">Worth thinking about before you rely on one:</p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
        <li>They generally work by scraping, not through the official API, since the official API does not permit it for videos you do not own. That places them outside YouTube's terms of service, which means they break periodically when YouTube changes something.</li>
        <li>Terms of service still apply to the content. A transcript is the creator's work. Reading it, quoting it, or using it for research is normal. Republishing someone's full transcript as your own content is not, regardless of how you obtained it.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 leading-relaxed mt-4">If you need transcripts occasionally, the built-in panel does the job. If you need them at scale for your own videos, Studio and the API are the supported routes.</p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white mt-10 mb-4">When there is no transcript at all</h2>
      <p class="text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">Some videos genuinely have no captions — the creator disabled them, or YouTube's speech recognition does not support the language.</p>
      <p class="text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 leading-relaxed mt-4">Your options in that case:</p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
        <li><strong>Request captions from the creator.</strong> For anything educational or professional, a polite message often works. Many creators simply have not thought about it.</li>
        <li><strong>Transcribe the audio yourself.</strong> Speech-to-text tools have become good enough that this is practical for a single video. Accuracy varies with audio quality, accent, and background noise.</li>
        <li><strong>Check if the content exists elsewhere.</strong> Conference talks, lectures, and podcast episodes are frequently published with official transcripts on the organiser's own site — often better ones than YouTube would have generated.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white mt-10 mb-4">Why transcripts are worth having</h2>
      <p class="text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">Beyond accessibility, which is the main reason:</p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-4">
        <li><strong>Reading is faster than watching.</strong> A 20-minute video is roughly 3,000 words — about eight minutes of reading. For research or note-taking, the transcript is simply more efficient.</li>
        <li><strong>Search within content.</strong> Ctrl+F through a transcript finds the moment you need instantly. Scrubbing a timeline does not.</li>
        <li><strong>Repurposing, if it is your own video.</strong> <cite class="text-slate-500 dark:text-slate-400 not-italic">A single 20-minute video can become a blog post, a newsletter issue, social clips, and show notes — the text is the raw material for all of it.</cite></li>
        <li><strong>Search visibility.</strong> <cite class="text-slate-500 dark:text-slate-400 not-italic">Search engines cannot watch video but can read text, so publishing full transcripts alongside video content has been a ranking factor for years.</cite> If you publish video on your own site, the transcript is free indexable content.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white mt-10 mb-4">Common questions</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
        <li><strong>Why is the Show transcript button missing?</strong><br><cite class="text-slate-500 dark:text-slate-400 not-italic">Captions may be disabled by the creator, the app version may hide the feature, or captions may not exist for that video.</cite> Live streams in progress and very recent uploads also lack them.</li>
        <li><strong>Are auto-generated transcripts accurate?</strong><br>Reasonably, for clear speech in well-supported languages. Accuracy drops with strong accents, technical vocabulary, proper nouns, multiple speakers, and background noise. Auto-captions also lack punctuation quality and speaker labels. Always proofread before using one for anything that matters.</li>
        <li><strong>Can I get a transcript with timestamps removed?</strong><br>Yes — toggle timestamps off inside the transcript panel before copying. You will still typically need to fix line breaks manually, since the copied text arrives broken at caption boundaries rather than at sentence ends.</li>
        <li><strong>Does the transcript match the captions exactly?</strong><br>Usually, since both come from the same caption track. If a creator uploaded a professionally written caption file, the transcript reflects it. If it is auto-generated, transcript and captions share the same errors.</li>
        <li><strong>Can I download transcripts in bulk?</strong><br>Not through the YouTube interface, and not through the official API for videos you do not own. For your own channel, Studio handles them one at a time, and the API can automate it.</li>
        <li><strong>Is it legal to use someone's transcript?</strong><br>Reading, quoting, and citing it is ordinary use. Republishing a full transcript as your own content is a copyright question, not a technical one — the words belong to whoever spoke them. When in doubt, quote briefly and link to the source.</li>
      </ul>

